-- allium: 3
--
-- Poodl — playing a game
--
-- Scope
--   One game of Poodl: how it starts, how letters are entered, how a guess is
--   scored, how the game ends, and what a player may abandon. This is the
--   core module; the other modules hang off it.
--
-- Includes
--   - The four modes, and the lifecycle of a single Game.
--   - The guess-scoring contract: two-pass duplicate handling, stated
--     precisely enough to test against.
--   - Input rules: letter entry, deletion, submission, and the two kinds of
--     rejection that consume no attempt.
--   - Per-letter keyboard knowledge derived from the guesses so far.
--   - Abandonment: what happens to the outgoing game when a new one starts.
--   - The endless-mode auto-continue countdown.
--   - Starting a game from nothing: the game-navigation surface, and the
--     first visit starting a random game by itself.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Where an answer comes from for random and endless games (the no-repeat
--     answer pool lives in statistics.allium) and for custom games (the link
--     decoding lives in sharing.allium). Both supply an answer by emitting
--     AnswerDrawn; this module creates the game from it.
--   - Anything counted or remembered across games (statistics.allium).
--   - Where the player's settings are stored and changed (settings.allium).
--     This module reads only what it needs to play, through the PlaySettings
--     placeholder below.
--   - Screen layout, markup, styling and animation mechanics.
--
-- Dependencies
--   words.allium — the Word entity and the two lists.
--   The external PlaySettings entity below is the abstraction this module
--   depends on for the two settings that change play. settings.allium's
--   Settings entity is what fulfils it.

use "./words.allium" as words

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    -- Poodl runs on one device for one person. There are no accounts, so the
    -- player exists by declaration rather than by being created.
    player: Player

    -- The settings that change how a game plays, read live: a change to hard
    -- mode applies from the very next submission.
    play_settings: PlaySettings
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The two settings that reach into play. Declared here as an abstraction so
-- that this module does not depend on settings.allium; settings.allium
-- depends on this one, and its Settings entity supplies these fields.
external entity PlaySettings {
    -- Hard mode: revealed letters must be reused. Read at submission time,
    -- not at the start of the game.
    hard_mode: Boolean

    -- Whether keys pressed on a physical keyboard reach the board.
    physical_keyboard: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One letter of one submitted guess, with what that letter turned out to be
-- worth. Positions are 1-based and run left to right.
value LetterResult {
    position: Integer
    letter: String
    mark: LetterMark
}

-- What is known about one letter of the alphabet across the whole game, as
-- shown on the on-screen keyboard. An absent status means "not yet seen in
-- any submitted guess".
value KeyKnowledge {
    letter: String
    status: LetterMark?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Scoring one guess against the answer. This is the heart of the game and the
-- place duplicate letters are easy to get wrong, so the two passes are stated
-- as obligations rather than left to the implementation.
contract GuessScoring {
    score_guess: (guess: words/Word, answer: words/Word) -> List<LetterResult>

    @invariant OneResultPerPositionInOrder
        -- The result carries exactly one LetterResult for each position
        -- 1..words/config.word_length, in ascending position order, and each
        -- result's letter is the guess's letter at that position.

    @invariant ExactMatchesClaimOccurrencesFirst
        -- Pass one visits every position. Where the guess letter equals the
        -- answer letter at that position, the position is marked correct and
        -- claims one occurrence of that letter in the answer. No position is
        -- marked present or absent during pass one.

    @invariant RemainingPositionsMarkedLeftToRight
        -- Pass two visits the positions left unmarked by pass one, in
        -- ascending position order. A position is marked present when the
        -- answer still holds an unclaimed occurrence of that position's
        -- letter, and claiming it; otherwise the position is marked absent.
        -- Order matters: with two of a letter in the guess and one in the
        -- answer, the leftmost unmatched position takes the present mark.

    @invariant MarkBudgetNeverExceeded
        -- For any letter, the number of correct and present marks in a single
        -- guess never exceeds the number of times that letter occurs in the
        -- answer. Every other position holding that letter is absent.

    @invariant AllCorrectExactlyWhenEqual
        -- Every position is marked correct if and only if the guess is the
        -- same word as the answer.

    @invariant Deterministic
        -- The same guess and answer always produce the same results.

    @guidance
        -- The pass-one-then-pass-two shape is not an implementation
        -- suggestion, it is the definition. A single left-to-right pass gives
        -- different, wrong answers for guesses that repeat a letter the
        -- answer holds once.
}

-- Whether the guesses already submitted in a game would all have been legal
-- under hard mode. Used when the player asks to turn hard mode on mid-game.
contract HardModeAdmission {
    respects_hard_mode: (guesses: Set<Guess>) -> Boolean

    @invariant PrefixSemantics
        -- True when every submitted guess satisfies the hard-mode constraints
        -- revealed by the guesses that preceded it, taking the guesses in
        -- ascending position order. A guess is judged only against what was
        -- known before it was submitted, never against its own results.

    @invariant TriviallyTrueWithNoGuesses
        -- An empty set of guesses respects hard mode.

    @invariant Deterministic
        -- The same guesses always produce the same verdict.
}

-- The state of the on-screen keyboard, derived from the guesses so far.
contract KeyboardKnowledge {
    keyboard_knowledge: (guesses: Set<Guess>) -> List<KeyKnowledge>

    @invariant CoversTheAlphabet
        -- Produces exactly one entry per letter of the English alphabet,
        -- whether or not that letter has been guessed.

    @invariant BestKnownStatusWins
        -- Each entry's status is the strongest mark that letter has received
        -- in any submitted guess, ranked correct over present over absent,
        -- and absent-of-value (null) when the letter has not been submitted.
        -- Knowledge never weakens: a letter shown correct stays correct even
        -- if a later guess places it wrongly.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum GameMode { random | endless | practice | custom }

enum LetterMark { correct | present | absent }

enum GuessRejectionReason { incomplete | not_in_dictionary | hard_mode_violation }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The person at this device. One player, one game at a time.
entity Player {
    -- The game on the board: in progress, or just finished and not yet
    -- replaced. Null before the first game.
    current_game: Game?

    -- Relationships
    games: Game with player = this

    -- Projections
    in_progress_games: games where status = in_progress

    invariant OnlyTheCurrentGameIsInProgress {
        in_progress_games.all(g => g = current_game)
    }
}

entity Game {
    player: Player
    mode: GameMode
    answer: words/Word
    status: in_progress | won | lost | abandoned

    -- Whether hard mode was on when this game began. Recorded for the record;
    -- submission is judged against the live setting, not against this.
    hard_mode_at_start: Boolean

    -- The letters typed but not yet submitted, between zero and word_length
    -- of them.
    current_input: String

    started_at: Timestamp
    completed_at: Timestamp when status = won | lost | abandoned

    -- When set, an endless game's next round starts at this moment unless the
    -- player stops the countdown first. Null in every other mode and whenever
    -- no countdown is running.
    auto_continue_at: Timestamp?

    transitions status {
        in_progress -> won
        in_progress -> lost
        in_progress -> abandoned
        terminal: won, lost, abandoned
    }

    -- Relationships
    guesses: Guess with game = this

    -- Derived: the game's state
    is_current: player.current_game = this
    attempts_used: guesses.count
    attempts_remaining: config.max_attempts - guesses.count
    is_complete: status in {won, lost, abandoned}
    is_finished_by_play: status in {won, lost}
    is_stat_eligible: mode in {random, endless}
    input_length: length(current_input)
    is_input_complete: input_length = words/config.word_length

    -- Derived: what the guesses so far have revealed
    all_results: flatMap(guesses, g => g.results)
    correct_results: filter(all_results, r => r.mark = correct)
    present_results: filter(all_results, r => r.mark = present)
    keyboard: keyboard_knowledge(guesses)

    -- The strongest mark a letter has earned anywhere in this game, or null
    -- when the letter has not been submitted. This is what the on-screen
    -- keyboard shows, and the precedence is correct over present over absent
    -- over nothing.
    letter_status(l):
        if filter(all_results, r => r.letter = l).any(r => r.mark = correct): correct
        else if filter(all_results, r => r.letter = l).any(r => r.mark = present): present
        else if filter(all_results, r => r.letter = l).any(r => r.mark = absent): absent
        else: null

    -- Hard mode, applied to a candidate guess: every letter revealed in place
    -- must be played in that same place, and every letter revealed as present
    -- must be played somewhere.
    satisfies_hard_mode(candidate):
        correct_results.all(r => letter_at(candidate.text, r.position) = r.letter)
        and present_results.all(r => contains_letter(candidate.text, r.letter))

    -- Whether hard mode may be turned on now without invalidating history.
    hard_mode_admissible: respects_hard_mode(guesses)

    invariant NeverMoreThanTheAttemptLimit {
        guesses.count <= config.max_attempts
    }

    invariant GuessPositionsAreDistinct {
        for a in guesses:
            for b in guesses:
                a != b implies a.position != b.position
    }
}

-- A guess the player has submitted, with what it revealed. Unsubmitted
-- letters live in Game.current_input and are not a Guess.
entity Guess {
    game: Game
    position: Integer
    word: words/Word
    results: List<LetterResult>

    is_winning: results.all(r => r.mark = correct)

    invariant PositionIsAnAttemptNumber {
        position >= 1 and position <= config.max_attempts
    }

    invariant OneResultPerLetter {
        results.count = words/config.word_length
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    max_attempts: Integer = 6

    -- How long the end-of-game modal waits before starting the next endless
    -- round. See the open question below.
    endless_countdown: Duration = 5.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Starting a game -------------------------------------------------------

-- Opening Poodl with nothing on the board starts a random game, so a first
-- visit lands on something playable rather than on a choice.
--
-- The guard is what makes this rule the whole of the resume story too. Open
-- Poodl with a game in progress and no rule fires: the board comes back as it
-- was left. Open it with a finished game still current and again nothing
-- fires, so the end-of-game modal is still there, answer and all.
--
-- PlayerOpensPoodl is deliberately the one trigger no surface provides:
-- opening the app is an arrival, not a control, and every surface is
-- something reached by arriving.
rule StartFirstGameOnOpening {
    when: PlayerOpensPoodl(player)

    requires: player.current_game = null

    ensures: NewGameRequested(player: player, mode: random)
}

-- Practice draws straight from the answer list: no pool, no record, repeats
-- allowed. Random and endless are answered by statistics.allium, which draws
-- from the no-repeat pool. Custom games never arrive this way; they arrive as
-- an AnswerDrawn emitted by sharing.allium when a link is opened.
rule ProvidePracticeAnswer {
    when: NewGameRequested(player, mode)

    requires: mode = practice

    let candidates = filter(words/Words, w => w.in_answer_list)
    let drawn = uniform_choice(candidates)

    ensures: AnswerDrawn(player: player, mode: mode, answer: drawn)

    @guidance
        -- uniform_choice is the one deliberately non-deterministic step in
        -- this specification. What matters is that every candidate is equally
        -- likely and that the result is a member of the candidates, not that
        -- the same input yields the same word.
}

-- Every game begins here, whatever supplied the answer. The outgoing game is
-- retired first: this is the single place abandonment can happen, so New
-- Game, switching mode, opening a custom link and the endless countdown all
-- get the same treatment.
rule BeginGame {
    when: AnswerDrawn(player, mode, answer)

    let outgoing = player.current_game

    ensures:
        if exists outgoing:
            RetireGame(game: outgoing)
        let game = Game.created(
            player: player,
            mode: mode,
            answer: answer,
            status: in_progress,
            hard_mode_at_start: play_settings.hard_mode,
            current_input: "",
            started_at: now
        )
        player.current_game = game
}

-- Abandoning a game that was under way counts as a loss. statistics.allium
-- chains from the transition; it decides whether this game was eligible.
rule AbandonRetiredGame {
    when: RetireGame(game)

    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.guesses.count >= 1

    ensures:
        game.status = abandoned
        game.completed_at = now
}

-- A game the player never guessed in leaves no trace at all: no loss, no
-- broken streak, nothing counted.
rule DiscardRetiredGame {
    when: RetireGame(game)

    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.guesses.count = 0

    ensures: not exists game
}

-- Entering letters ------------------------------------------------------

rule PlayerEntersLetter {
    when: PlayerEntersLetter(player, letter)

    let game = player.current_game

    requires: exists game
    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: is_letter(letter)
    requires: game.input_length < words/config.word_length

    ensures: game.current_input = append(game.current_input, lowercase(letter))
}

rule PlayerDeletesLetter {
    when: PlayerDeletesLetter(player)

    let game = player.current_game

    requires: exists game
    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.input_length > 0

    ensures: game.current_input = drop_last(game.current_input)
}

-- Submitting a guess ----------------------------------------------------

-- Fewer than five letters. Nothing is scored and no attempt is spent.
rule RejectIncompleteGuess {
    when: PlayerSubmitsGuess(player)

    let game = player.current_game

    requires: exists game
    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.input_length < words/config.word_length

    ensures: GuessRejected(game: game, reason: incomplete)
}

-- Five letters, but not a word Poodl accepts.
rule RejectUnknownWord {
    when: PlayerSubmitsGuess(player)

    let game = player.current_game

    requires: exists game
    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.is_input_complete

    let input = game.current_input
    let candidate = words/Word{text: input}

    requires: not exists candidate or not candidate.in_guess_dictionary

    ensures: GuessRejected(game: game, reason: not_in_dictionary)
}

-- A real word, but it drops a letter the player has already been shown. Hard
-- mode is read live, so this depends on the setting as it stands now.
rule RejectHardModeViolation {
    when: PlayerSubmitsGuess(player)

    let game = player.current_game

    requires: exists game
    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.is_input_complete

    let input = game.current_input
    let candidate = words/Word{text: input}

    requires: exists candidate
    requires: candidate.in_guess_dictionary
    requires: play_settings.hard_mode
    requires: not game.satisfies_hard_mode(candidate)

    ensures: GuessRejected(game: game, reason: hard_mode_violation)
}

-- The guess stands: it is scored, it spends an attempt, and it may end the
-- game.
rule AcceptGuess {
    when: PlayerSubmitsGuess(player)

    let game = player.current_game

    requires: exists game
    requires: game.status = in_progress
    requires: game.is_input_complete

    let input = game.current_input
    let candidate = words/Word{text: input}
    let attempt = game.attempts_used + 1

    requires: exists candidate
    requires: candidate.in_guess_dictionary
    requires: not play_settings.hard_mode or game.satisfies_hard_mode(candidate)

    ensures:
        let submitted = Guess.created(
            game: game,
            position: attempt,
            word: candidate,
            results: score_guess(candidate, game.answer)
        )
        game.current_input = ""
        if submitted.is_winning:
            game.status = won
            game.completed_at = now
        else if attempt = config.max_attempts:
            game.status = lost
            game.completed_at = now
}

-- Endless mode ----------------------------------------------------------

-- Endless shows the same end-of-game modal as random, but arms a countdown.
rule ArmEndlessCountdown {
    when: game: Game.is_finished_by_play

    requires: game.mode = endless
    requires: game.auto_continue_at = null

    ensures: game.auto_continue_at = now + config.endless_countdown
}

-- The countdown runs out and the next round starts by itself. Clearing
-- auto_continue_at is what stops this firing again.
rule EndlessCountdownElapses {
    when: game: Game.auto_continue_at <= now

    requires: game.mode = endless
    requires: game.is_current

    ensures:
        game.auto_continue_at = null
        NewGameRequested(player: game.player, mode: endless)
}

-- The player stops the countdown and the modal simply stays put. Starting the
-- next round is then up to them, exactly as in random mode.
rule PlayerStopsCountdown {
    when: PlayerStopsCountdown(player, game)

    requires: game.auto_continue_at != null

    ensures: game.auto_continue_at = null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The duplicate-letter obligation, stated over stored state rather than over
-- the scoring function: a letter is never marked correct or present more
-- times in one guess than it occurs in the answer.
invariant MarksNeverExceedAnswerOccurrences {
    for g in Guesses:
        for r in g.results:
            filter(g.results, x => x.letter = r.letter and x.mark in {correct, present}).count
                <= occurrences(g.game.answer.text, r.letter)
}

invariant CorrectMarksSitOnMatchingPositions {
    for g in Guesses:
        for r in g.results:
            r.mark = correct implies letter_at(g.game.answer.text, r.position) = r.letter
}

invariant LettersNotInTheAnswerAreMarkedAbsent {
    for g in Guesses:
        for r in g.results:
            occurrences(g.game.answer.text, r.letter) = 0 implies r.mark = absent
}

invariant WinningGuessIsTheAnswer {
    for g in Guesses:
        g.is_winning implies g.word = g.game.answer
}

invariant WonGamesHoldAWinningGuess {
    for g in Games:
        g.status = won implies g.guesses.any(x => x.is_winning)
}

invariant LostGamesUsedEveryAttempt {
    for g in Games:
        g.status = lost implies g.guesses.count = config.max_attempts
}

invariant AbandonedGamesHadAGuess {
    for g in Games:
        g.status = abandoned implies g.guesses.count >= 1
}

-- The keyboard never contradicts the guesses. The other direction, that every
-- letter of the alphabet has an entry, is an obligation on the
-- KeyboardKnowledge contract.
invariant KeyboardAgreesWithTheGuesses {
    for g in Games:
        for key in g.keyboard:
            key.status != null implies key.status = g.letter_status(key.letter)
}

invariant AnswersAreAlwaysGuessable {
    for g in Games:
        g.answer.in_guess_dictionary
}

invariant InputNeverExceedsWordLength {
    for g in Games:
        g.input_length <= words/config.word_length
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Starting and switching games. This is the only surface that is not scoped
-- to a game, so it is the one that exists before the first game and the one
-- that can always start another.
surface GameNavigation {
    facing viewer: Player

    exposes:
        viewer.current_game?.mode
        viewer.current_game?.status

    provides:
        NewGameRequested(viewer, mode)

    related:
        GameBoard(viewer.current_game)
            when viewer.current_game != null

    @guarantee AvailableWhetherOrNotAGameExists
        -- Nothing here depends on a game being under way. On a first visit,
        -- with no game and no history, this surface is still present and can
        -- still start one.

    @guarantee OpeningPoodlWithNoGameStartsARandomOne
        -- A player arriving with nothing on the board does not have to choose
        -- a mode first: a random game is already waiting. Arriving with a
        -- game in progress resumes it untouched, and arriving with a finished
        -- game shows its conclusion again rather than moving on.

    @guarantee ThreeModesCanBeStartedFromHere
        -- Random, endless and practice are the modes a player can start.
        -- Custom is not among them: a custom game exists only because someone
        -- made a link, so it is reached by opening that link and never by
        -- choosing a mode.

    @guarantee StartingAGameEndsTheOneUnderWay
        -- Asking for a new game, in the same mode or a different one, retires
        -- whatever was on the board. If that game was under way and had a
        -- guess in it, it counts as a loss in a mode that counts; if it had no
        -- guesses, it goes without trace. This is stated where the player acts
        -- on it, so the cost of switching mode mid-game is never a surprise.

    @guarantee CurrentModeIsPerceivable
        -- Which mode is being played, and whether a game is under way at all,
        -- are readable as text rather than signalled only by which control
        -- looks selected.

    @guarantee FullyKeyboardOperable
        -- Every mode and the New Game control can be reached and invoked from
        -- the keyboard alone, and the currently selected mode is announced.
}

-- The board. It exists for as long as a game is on it, including after the
-- game ends: the final grid stays visible behind the end-of-game modal.
surface GameBoard {
    facing viewer: Player

    context game: Game where player = viewer and is_current

    exposes:
        game.mode
        game.attempts_used
        game.attempts_remaining
        game.current_input
        for submitted in game.guesses:
            submitted.position
            submitted.word.text
            submitted.results
        for key in game.keyboard:
            key.letter
            key.status

    provides:
        PlayerEntersLetter(viewer, letter)
            when game.status = in_progress and game.input_length < words/config.word_length
        PlayerDeletesLetter(viewer)
            when game.status = in_progress and game.input_length > 0
        PlayerSubmitsGuess(viewer)
            when game.status = in_progress
        NewGameRequested(viewer, mode)

    related:
        PhysicalKeyboardInput(game)
            when game.status = in_progress
        GameConclusion(game)
            when game.is_finished_by_play

    @guarantee AnswerIsNeverExposedWhileInProgress
        -- game.answer is deliberately absent from the exposed set. While the
        -- game is in progress nothing on this surface reveals the answer, or
        -- any letter of it, beyond what the player's own submitted guesses
        -- have revealed. The answer becomes visible only on GameConclusion,
        -- once the game is over. This holds for custom games too: the
        -- creator's word is hidden the moment the game starts.

    @guarantee ResultsAreNeverConveyedByColourAlone
        -- Every letter result and every keyboard key status is distinguishable
        -- without colour vision: correct, present and absent each carry a
        -- non-colour indication as well as their colour.

    @guarantee EverySubmittedGuessIsAnnounced
        -- When a guess is accepted, its per-letter results are announced to
        -- assistive technology in reading order, along with the attempt
        -- number and how many attempts remain.

    @guarantee EveryRejectionIsAnnounced
        -- A GuessRejected outcome is made perceivable both visually and to
        -- assistive technology, and says which of the three reasons applied.
        -- A rejected guess spends no attempt: the typed letters stay on the
        -- board for the player to correct.

    @guarantee FullyKeyboardOperable
        -- Every operation on this surface can be reached and invoked from the
        -- keyboard alone, including each key of the on-screen keyboard. This
        -- holds regardless of the physical_keyboard setting, which governs
        -- only whether typing goes straight into the board.

    @guarantee InProgressGameSurvivesReload
        -- The current game — its mode, its answer, its submitted guesses and
        -- their results, and any unsubmitted letters — persists between
        -- sessions on the same device and browser. Reopening Poodl resumes
        -- the game exactly where it was left.

    @guarantee MotionRespectsTheReducedMotionPreference
        -- Tile and key animations run only when the animations setting is on
        -- and the operating system expresses no reduced-motion preference.
        -- The operating system preference wins.
}

-- Typing straight into the board. The same three actions as the on-screen
-- keyboard, from a different channel, and the only one the physical_keyboard
-- setting can switch off.
surface PhysicalKeyboardInput {
    facing viewer: Player

    context game: Game
        where player = viewer and is_current and status = in_progress

    let physical_input_enabled = play_settings.physical_keyboard

    provides:
        PlayerEntersLetter(viewer, letter)
            when physical_input_enabled and game.input_length < words/config.word_length
        PlayerDeletesLetter(viewer)
            when physical_input_enabled and game.input_length > 0
        PlayerSubmitsGuess(viewer)
            when physical_input_enabled

    @guarantee EnterSubmitsAndBackspaceDeletes
        -- Enter submits the current input and Backspace deletes its last
        -- letter. Letter keys enter that letter, whatever case they arrive in.

    @guarantee TurningThisOffLeavesTheGameFullyPlayable
        -- With physical_keyboard off, the on-screen keyboard still offers
        -- every action, and remains reachable by keyboard navigation.
}

-- The end-of-game modal. Random waits here indefinitely; endless counts down
-- and moves on unless the player stops it.
surface GameConclusion {
    facing viewer: Player

    context game: Game
        where player = viewer and is_current and is_finished_by_play

    exposes:
        game.status
        game.mode
        game.answer.text
        game.attempts_used
        game.auto_continue_at
        for submitted in game.guesses:
            submitted.position
            submitted.word.text
            submitted.results

    provides:
        NewGameRequested(viewer, mode)
        PlayerStopsCountdown(viewer, game)
            when game.auto_continue_at != null

    related:
        GameBoard(game)

    @guarantee OutcomeAnswerAndAttemptsAreAllShown
        -- Whether the game was won or lost, the answer, and how many attempts
        -- were used are all shown, on a win as well as on a loss.

    @guarantee EndlessContinuesUnlessStopped
        -- In endless mode the next game starts by itself once the countdown
        -- elapses. The remaining time is perceivable, and stopping the
        -- countdown is an action the player can take at any point while it
        -- runs. In every other mode nothing happens until the player asks
        -- for a new game.

    @guarantee NoDailyLimit
        -- A new game can always be requested. Poodl never withholds a game
        -- because of the date or because of how many have been played.

    @guarantee ConclusionIsAnnounced
        -- The outcome, the answer and the attempt count are announced to
        -- assistive technology when the game ends, and an armed countdown is
        -- announced along with the means to stop it.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "First visit starts a random game automatically, on the reading that a Wordle-like should open onto a playable board rather than onto a menu. Is that wanted, or should a first-time player be asked to choose a mode? The choice also decides what a player who has cleared their browser data meets on their way back in."

open question "Endless countdown duration - endless_countdown defaults to 5 seconds. Is that the right amount of time to read the answer before the next game takes over? It interacts with the ConclusionIsAnnounced guarantee: a screen reader may not finish the announcement in five seconds, which argues for a longer default or for the countdown pausing while an announcement is in flight."

open question "Stopping the countdown - decision 2 describes the player pausing or exiting the countdown, which reads as two behaviours; this specification models one action that cancels it outright. Should a stopped countdown be resumable, and should exiting mean something different from pausing?"

open question "hard_mode_at_start is recorded on every game but no rule reads it, because hard mode is judged live at submission. Should it instead lock hard mode for the duration of a game, or is the field only there for the record?"

open question "Retention of finished games - a won or lost game stays on the board until a new one replaces it, and nothing removes it afterwards. Should finished games be discarded once they are no longer current, or is a history of past games wanted (it would need its own scope: what is kept, and for how long)?"

open question "Whether a player may request a mode that needs an answer nobody supplies. NewGameRequested carries a mode, and only practice, random and endless have a rule that answers it; a request for custom produces no game at all, silently. Should that be a stated rejection instead?"
